  "updated": "2026-09-18T16:27:18.760788+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nA stalled frontal boundary remains active with convection across the \nOhio Valley this morning but SE MI will see increasing drying \ninfluence from high pressure passing through northern Ontario today. \nNortheast flow allows remaining stratus to push south of the \nstateline with a good amount of sunshine for the area through \nmidday. Subtle negative thickness advection within the southern \nextent of thermal troughing allows high temps to arrive right around \nseasonal average in the lower 70s and upper 60s (downwind of Lake \nHuron).\n\nLow pressure emerges out of the High Plains today in response to a \nshortwave and upper jet entrance region running the ridge axis in \nplace, bolstered by a feed of monsoonal moisture from the Four \nCorners region. Will see the first signs of this system locally \nlater this afternoon as high cloud begins to spill in from the west. \nDry weather is most likely to prevail overnight as persistent \neasterly flow maintains a dry pocket within the lowest 8 kft or so. \nUpstream, organized moisture transport into the developing warm \nfrontal slope will produce a corridor of steady and at times heavy \nrainfall. The LLJ driving this rain is shown to veer overnight \nbefore beginning to target parts of Lower MI Saturday morning. There \nare still some discrepancies noted in the strength and northeastward \nextent of this feature among 00z guidance, which appears to govern \nhow far north and east the swath of rain reaches into the local area \non Saturday.\n\nModel cross sections show a sharp 850-700mb frontal structure and \nattendant fgen within deep layer saturation, suggestive of banded \nprecip with inherent uncertainty as to where the frontal slope will \nactivate and for how long at any given location. This is evidenced \nby a 1+\" spread between the 25th and 75th percentile QPF in LREF \ngrand ensemble membership. QPF output in coarser global model \nsolutions is likely overdone spatially and prefer the look of hi-res \nsolutions that can better account for the initial dry easterly flow \nand some of the mesoscale processes involved. Overall flavor of \nthese solutions shows the heaviest rain sinking southeast across \nLake Michigan and Chicagoland into western Lower MI and northern IN \nSaturday morning before the LLJ weakens and rates/coverage begin to \ntaper off midday. What's left for SE MI is a cool, cloudy, and \nshowery pattern with amounts most probable to hold below a half inch \nthrough 00z Sun. Rates could become moderate at times as PWAT rises \nabove 1.50\", but the threat for widespread heavier rain is low. Weak \nlapse rates and marginal elevated instability offer low potential \nfor an embedded thunderstorm or two in the south. Conversely, \nportions of the area north of I-69 may see little to no rain given \nthe farther proximity from the feed of moisture and lift.\n\nAdditional showers are likely, especially south of M-59, Saturday \nnight into Sunday. The stalled frontal slope remains overhead while \nadditional moisture continues to feed along it. As the nocturnal LLJ \nbecomes active again over the northern Ohio Valley, this will be a \nwindow for training showers that have potential to produce locally \nover 1 inch of rain. Placement of the axis of heaviest rain still \ncarries high uncertainty and may yet be displaced south of the state \nline. Additional clarity should come into focus today and tonight as \nadditional hi-res runs begin to include this period.\n\nHigh pressure builds back into northern Ontario on Sunday, offering \nanother period of dry northeast flow that should shunt any lingering \nshowers southward by late in the day. A tightening gradient brings \nbreezy conditions with temperatures capped in the mid 60s. Cool and \nmostly cloudy conditions carry into the early week as the stalled \nfront remains active over the Ohio Valley. Showers will be possible \nat times, especially in the south. An upper low looks to develop \noverhead midweek which brings higher likelihood for showers \nWednesday and Thursday.",
